1
00:00:01,100 --> 00:00:04,510
Why has Warren Buffett held Coca-Cola for 38 years without selling?

2
00:00:05,310 --> 00:00:07,301
The answer takes one calculator tap.

3
00:00:07,301 --> 00:00:10,840
We'll also work out what dividend you'd get if you bought today.

4
00:00:11,640 --> 00:00:14,241
Three seconds to pick a can from the fridge.

5
00:00:14,241 --> 00:00:15,009
In that time,

6
00:00:15,009 --> 00:00:18,970
Buffett's company collects about 80 dollars in Coca-Cola dividends.

7
00:00:19,770 --> 00:00:23,932
27 dollars a second, about 2.3 million a day,

8
00:00:23,932 --> 00:00:25,690
848 million a year.

9
00:00:26,490 --> 00:00:31,690
Yet all Buffett ever paid for this stake was 1.3 billion dollars,

10
00:00:31,690 --> 00:00:33,450
between 1988 and 1994.

11
00:00:33,650 --> 00:00:36,240
Since then, not even the price of another can.

12
00:00:37,040 --> 00:00:38,943
But here's the stranger part.

13
00:00:38,943 --> 00:00:40,714
Buffett sold Coke as a boy,

14
00:00:40,714 --> 00:00:43,010
yet took 52 years to buy the stock.

15
00:00:43,210 --> 00:00:45,041
The story starts in 1936,

16
00:00:45,041 --> 00:00:47,530
in a small grocery store in Omaha.

17
00:00:49,230 --> 00:00:50,470
Part one.

18
00:00:51,270 --> 00:00:52,807
Omaha, Nebraska, 1936.

19
00:00:52,807 --> 00:00:55,251
At his grandfather's grocery store,

20
00:00:55,251 --> 00:00:58,464
six-year-old Warren Buffett started buying six

21
00:00:58,464 --> 00:01:00,490
bottles of Coke for 25 cents.

22
00:01:00,690 --> 00:01:03,780
Then he sold them around the neighborhood for 5 cents each.

23
00:01:04,570 --> 00:01:09,167
Sell all six and you have 30 cents: 5 cents of profit,

24
00:01:09,167 --> 00:01:11,210
20% on a 25-cent outlay.

25
00:01:11,410 --> 00:01:13,852
Grandpa's store, neighborhood customers,

26
00:01:13,852 --> 00:01:15,073
25 cents of capital.

27
00:01:15,073 --> 00:01:17,210
A very Buffett-like first business.

28
00:01:18,020 --> 00:01:19,421
In his 1989 letter,

29
00:01:19,421 --> 00:01:22,740
Buffett looked back: even then he clearly saw

30
00:01:22,740 --> 00:01:26,280
the product's extraordinary appeal to customers.

31
00:01:26,470 --> 00:01:29,209
What the boy saw: a good business is one where

32
00:01:29,209 --> 00:01:31,710
the same customer comes back to buy again.

33
00:01:32,510 --> 00:01:35,319
Yet the boy who saw this then just watched

34
00:01:35,319 --> 00:01:37,460
Coca-Cola for the next 52 years.

35
00:01:37,660 --> 00:01:38,686
In his own words,

36
00:01:38,686 --> 00:01:42,730
he watched Coke blanket the world and still overlooked the obvious.

37
00:01:44,430 --> 00:01:47,320
Part two. Let's go to 1988.

38
00:01:48,120 --> 00:01:50,823
Buffett wrote: only in the summer of 1988 did

39
00:01:50,823 --> 00:01:53,707
my brain finally establish contact with my eyes.

40
00:01:53,707 --> 00:01:55,930
He finally acted on what he had seen.

41
00:01:56,730 --> 00:01:59,279
That year, at 57, Buffett began buying.

42
00:01:59,279 --> 00:02:03,331
By early the next year he'd spent just over 1 billion dollars,

43
00:02:03,331 --> 00:02:04,900
for 6.3% of the company.

44
00:02:05,700 --> 00:02:09,692
By the numbers back then, it wasn't cheap.

45
00:02:09,692 --> 00:02:14,730
The stock traded at about 15 times a year's earnings.

46
00:02:14,940 --> 00:02:16,738
For every 10,000 won of stock,

47
00:02:16,738 --> 00:02:18,715
about 670 won of yearly earnings,

48
00:02:18,715 --> 00:02:19,913
while long-term U.S.

49
00:02:19,913 --> 00:02:22,430
Treasuries paid about 900 won in interest.

50
00:02:22,630 --> 00:02:24,297
He could have earned more just sitting in bonds,

51
00:02:24,297 --> 00:02:25,200
and still chose the stock.

52
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:28,438
And the stock had already risen an average of

53
00:02:28,438 --> 00:02:30,550
18% a year for the previous five years.

54
00:02:30,750 --> 00:02:32,951
Not a sale price. Full price,

55
00:02:32,951 --> 00:02:35,000
on the day the tag went up.

56
00:02:35,800 --> 00:02:38,104
Did Buffett misread the calculator? No.

57
00:02:38,104 --> 00:02:41,530
He was looking at something other than this year's profit.

58
00:02:43,240 --> 00:02:46,110
What he saw was how Coca-Cola makes money.

59
00:02:46,910 --> 00:02:48,111
In most countries,

60
00:02:48,111 --> 00:02:51,580
Coca-Cola doesn't bottle or truck the drinks itself.

61
00:02:51,780 --> 00:02:55,078
It sells concentrate; bottling partners do the

62
00:02:55,078 --> 00:02:57,730
heavy work of filling and delivering.

63
00:02:58,530 --> 00:03:00,765
Others buy the plants and trucks; Coca-Cola

64
00:03:00,765 --> 00:03:03,000
collects for the brand and the concentrate.

65
00:03:03,190 --> 00:03:06,336
So margins are thick: last quarter it kept 33

66
00:03:06,336 --> 00:03:10,390
dollars of operating profit on every 100 dollars of sales.

67
00:03:11,200 --> 00:03:14,455
Roberto Goizueta's team, in charge since 1981,

68
00:03:14,455 --> 00:03:17,640
was spreading this business across the world.

69
00:03:18,430 --> 00:03:19,626
Ten years later,

70
00:03:19,626 --> 00:03:22,766
Coca-Cola's net income was about 3.4 times

71
00:03:22,766 --> 00:03:27,400
larger: from 1 billion dollars in 1988 to 3.5 billion in 1998.

72
00:03:27,600 --> 00:03:28,590
That's part two.

73
00:03:28,590 --> 00:03:30,571
Expensive on that year's profit,

74
00:03:30,571 --> 00:03:33,667
but Buffett was pricing the profits still to come.

75
00:03:33,667 --> 00:03:35,400
For ten years, he was right.

76
00:03:36,200 --> 00:03:38,922
So was it smooth sailing after that?

77
00:03:38,922 --> 00:03:39,830
Not exactly.

78
00:03:41,530 --> 00:03:43,300
Let's go to 1998.

79
00:03:44,110 --> 00:03:46,005
After its 1998 surge,

80
00:03:46,005 --> 00:03:51,418
Coca-Cola stock only got back near that peak 16 years later,

81
00:03:51,418 --> 00:03:52,140
in 2014.

82
00:03:52,340 --> 00:03:55,070
Long enough for a newborn to reach high school.

83
00:03:55,870 --> 00:03:57,056
In his 2004 letter,

84
00:03:57,056 --> 00:03:59,740
Buffett called it a big mistake not to sell

85
00:03:59,740 --> 00:04:02,300
several large holdings during the bubble.

86
00:04:03,100 --> 00:04:04,311
So why not sell?

87
00:04:04,311 --> 00:04:08,700
Because one thing kept moving through those 16 flat years.

88
00:04:09,510 --> 00:04:12,511
The dividend: the cash a company hands its

89
00:04:12,511 --> 00:04:14,440
shareholders out of profit.

90
00:04:14,640 --> 00:04:19,070
Coca-Cola has raised it for 64 straight years since 1962.

91
00:04:19,070 --> 00:04:21,946
This year it pays 53 cents a quarter,

92
00:04:21,946 --> 00:04:23,500
2.12 dollars a year.

93
00:04:24,290 --> 00:04:27,517
Berkshire's Coca-Cola dividends grew from 75

94
00:04:27,517 --> 00:04:30,963
million dollars in 1994 to 704 million in 2022,

95
00:04:30,963 --> 00:04:33,310
and about 848 million this year.

96
00:04:33,510 --> 00:04:35,246
In his 2022 letter,

97
00:04:35,246 --> 00:04:39,450
Buffett said they rose as surely as birthdays.

98
00:04:39,650 --> 00:04:43,470
Birthdays come once a year; this company sends a gift four times.

99
00:04:44,270 --> 00:04:46,479
So what did 38 years of waiting earn?

100
00:04:46,479 --> 00:04:48,210
One multiplication tells you.

101
00:04:49,910 --> 00:04:51,040
Part three.

102
00:04:51,840 --> 00:04:56,423
By 1994 Berkshire held 100 million Coca-Cola shares.

103
00:04:56,423 --> 00:04:59,684
Two 2-for-1 splits, in 1996 and 2012,

104
00:04:59,684 --> 00:05:02,240
turned them into 400 million.

105
00:05:02,440 --> 00:05:05,045
1.3 billion dollars divided by 400 million:

106
00:05:05,045 --> 00:05:06,680
about 3.25 dollars a share.

107
00:05:07,490 --> 00:05:11,134
Each of those shares pays 2.12 dollars this year.

108
00:05:11,134 --> 00:05:13,960
400 million times 2.12 is 848 million.

109
00:05:14,160 --> 00:05:15,759
About 65% of what he paid.

110
00:05:15,759 --> 00:05:18,466
A dividend that was about 6% of cost in 1994

111
00:05:18,466 --> 00:05:21,910
now returns almost the whole stake in a year and a half.

112
00:05:22,110 --> 00:05:22,873
Same soda,

113
00:05:22,873 --> 00:05:26,080
but only the 1988 buyer gets free refills.

114
00:05:26,880 --> 00:05:29,267
But the 65% is partly an illusion.

115
00:05:29,267 --> 00:05:31,303
Sell for about $36.7 billion,

116
00:05:31,303 --> 00:05:33,690
put it into a stock yielding 2.3%,

117
00:05:33,690 --> 00:05:37,130
and the yearly dividend comes out about the same.

118
00:05:37,330 --> 00:05:39,147
So the real reasons are two.

119
00:05:39,147 --> 00:05:41,936
The gain is about $35.4 billion; 21% of it,

120
00:05:41,936 --> 00:05:43,169
about $7.4 billion,

121
00:05:43,169 --> 00:05:45,245
would go to corporate tax first,

122
00:05:45,245 --> 00:05:48,100
and what's left can't pay the same dividend.

123
00:05:48,290 --> 00:05:52,156
And a company customers return to like this is hard to find again.

124
00:05:52,156 --> 00:05:53,210
That's part three.

125
00:05:54,020 --> 00:05:56,049
One uncomfortable question remains.

126
00:05:56,049 --> 00:05:58,310
Would buying today tell the same story?

127
00:05:58,510 --> 00:05:59,186
Conveniently,

128
00:05:59,186 --> 00:06:01,940
the third-quarter report card came out on October 27.

129
00:06:03,630 --> 00:06:05,410
Let's look at the October 27 release.

130
00:06:06,210 --> 00:06:10,818
Third-quarter revenue rose 7% to 13.4 billion dollars.

131
00:06:10,818 --> 00:06:15,170
Comparable earnings per share rose 11% to 91 cents.

132
00:06:15,370 --> 00:06:16,817
Unit case volume grew 5%,

133
00:06:16,817 --> 00:06:19,480
while price and mix rose just 1%: it sold more

134
00:06:19,480 --> 00:06:20,927
without leaning on price.

135
00:06:20,927 --> 00:06:23,590
The company also raised its full-year outlook.

136
00:06:24,390 --> 00:06:28,554
Bottom line: buy today and there's no 65%.

137
00:06:28,554 --> 00:06:29,545
At $91.73,

138
00:06:29,545 --> 00:06:33,610
the yearly dividend is 2.3% of the price.

139
00:06:33,810 --> 00:06:35,488
On 100 million won of stock,

140
00:06:35,488 --> 00:06:38,124
that's about about 231만 원 a year before tax,

141
00:06:38,124 --> 00:06:40,760
and U.S. dividends arrive with 15% withheld.

142
00:06:41,560 --> 00:06:45,518
Here's the twist: today's numbers rhyme with 1988.

143
00:06:45,518 --> 00:06:49,477
The stock trades at about 30.2x a year's earnings,

144
00:06:49,477 --> 00:06:52,960
while the 10-year Treasury yields around 5%.

145
00:06:53,160 --> 00:06:55,972
On paper, bonds pay more per year again.

146
00:06:55,972 --> 00:07:00,470
We're standing at the same fridge Buffett stood at 38 years ago.

147
00:07:00,670 --> 00:07:05,019
So the one question to ask: can profits grow as

148
00:07:05,019 --> 00:07:07,610
fast as they did after 1988?

149
00:07:08,410 --> 00:07:11,263
Your yield on cost is this year's dividend

150
00:07:11,263 --> 00:07:13,300
divided by the price you paid.

151
00:07:13,500 --> 00:07:15,815
If the dividend grows 5% a year,

152
00:07:15,815 --> 00:07:19,360
in ten years it would be about 3.8% of your cost.

153
00:07:19,560 --> 00:07:20,663
On 100 million won,

154
00:07:20,663 --> 00:07:23,332
that's about about 376만 원 a year in ten years,

155
00:07:23,332 --> 00:07:23,970
before tax.

156
00:07:24,170 --> 00:07:28,450
Buffett's 65% is that same math run for 38 years.

157
00:07:29,240 --> 00:07:31,223
Of course this math could miss.

158
00:07:31,223 --> 00:07:35,510
Sugar taxes and the spread of obesity drugs could mean fewer sodas.

159
00:07:35,720 --> 00:07:37,631
A tax dispute with the IRS and weak

160
00:07:37,631 --> 00:07:40,360
emerging-market currencies could also cut profits.

161
00:07:41,160 --> 00:07:44,830
I jot these numbers down in a notebook every day; that's ValueNote.

162
00:07:45,030 --> 00:07:47,655
Coca-Cola's earning-power score is 86,

163
00:07:47,655 --> 00:07:49,383
but its price score is 5.

164
00:07:49,383 --> 00:07:53,390
A great company and a great price are different questions.

165
00:07:55,080 --> 00:07:56,140
Let's wrap up.

166
00:07:56,950 --> 00:07:59,047
1936: six bottles for 25 cents.

167
00:07:59,047 --> 00:08:01,686
1988: a purchase that looked expensive.

168
00:08:01,686 --> 00:08:04,730
1994: buying finished at 1.3 billion dollars.

169
00:08:04,930 --> 00:08:08,870
And 2026: 65% of the cost comes back every year.

170
00:08:09,660 --> 00:08:12,290
In his 1988 letter Buffett wrote: with

171
00:08:12,290 --> 00:08:15,820
outstanding businesses and outstanding managements,

172
00:08:15,820 --> 00:08:18,520
our favorite holding period is forever.

173
00:08:19,320 --> 00:08:22,119
So the question to keep isn't buy or sell.

174
00:08:22,119 --> 00:08:25,784
Do customers keep coming back to the companies you own,

175
00:08:25,784 --> 00:08:28,250
and do their profits grow every year?

176
00:08:28,450 --> 00:08:30,498
And by the way: while you watched this video,

177
00:08:30,498 --> 00:08:33,410
Berkshire collected about 15,000 dollars in Coca-Cola dividends.

178
00:08:34,220 --> 00:08:36,131
If you want more stories behind the numbers,

179
00:08:36,131 --> 00:08:37,131
please subscribe, like,

180
00:08:37,131 --> 00:08:38,260
and turn on notifications.

181
00:08:38,460 --> 00:08:40,868
The story of the companies Buffett picked in

182
00:08:40,868 --> 00:08:42,400
Japan is worth watching too.

183
00:08:42,600 --> 00:08:45,965
Which company you own keeps customers coming back?

184
00:08:45,965 --> 00:08:48,320
Tell us in the comments. Thank you.
